Consider the reaction
H_2(g) \longrightarrow H(g) + H(g), \Delta_2 H^0 = 436.4 \text{ kJ}
It shows that H-H bond in one mole of H_2(g) is decomposed producing gaseous H atoms. The enthalpy change of the reaction, 436.4 kJ is bond enthalpy of the H-H bond. The enthalpy change required to break particular covalent bond in one mole of gaseous molecule to produce gaseous atoms and/or radicals, is called bond enthalpy.
